            <ArticleTitle>Acute renal infarction from a cardiac thrombus.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>631-5</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">A 53-year-old man presented to hospital 2 hours after the abrupt onset of left upper abdominal pain. He was treated with analgesics and discharged after 4 hours of observation, but presented to another hospital 2 hours later with severe left abdominal pain. His past medical history included ischemic dilated cardiomyopathy due to recurrent myocardial infarction.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="INVESTIGATIONS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Physical examination, electrocardiography, laboratory investigations, contrast-enhanced computed tomography, and transesophageal echocardiography.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DIAGNOSIS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Renal artery thromboembolism resulting from dilated cardiomyopathy, severely reduced cardiac function and an intracardiac thrombus.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MANAGEMENT" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Anticoagulation with unfractionated heparin followed by enoxaparin and warfarin.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
